# Architecture rule

The code is moving to a modular monolith with one module per bounded context
(`docs/adr/0001-modular-monolith-by-bounded-context.md`). The map, the layers and the migration
are in `docs/engineering/architecture.md`; the rules with examples are in the `clean-architecture`
skill.

## Modules

A context lives in `src/modules/<context>/`. The folder names are fixed: `format-rules`,
`deck-building`, `deck-analysis`, `playtest`, `ai-copilot`, `card-catalog`, `combos`,
`meta-insights`, `deck-interchange`, `collection`, `community`, `identity`, `rules-reference`,
`site`. Layers exist only when the context needs them:

- `domain/`: readonly types and pure functions. No React, Next.js, Prisma, `fetch` or other I/O.
- `application/`: use cases, which depend on ports declared in `domain/` or `application/`.
- `infrastructure/`: Prisma repositories, HTTP clients, anti-corruption adapters. Server-only,
  except a browser API client.
- `client/`: the typed browser API client, Zustand stores and hooks.
- `ui/`: React components, kept small. A container composes presentational components; logic
  lives in hooks or the domain.

## Entry points (`docs/adr/0002-module-entry-points-index-server-client.md`)

- `index.ts`: pure domain types and functions, safe on the server, in the browser and in the
  middleware.
- `server.ts`: starts with `import "server-only"` and exports the wired use cases.
- `client.ts`: client-safe hooks, stores and the API client.

Other contexts import only these three files, or a component by its path under `ui/`. Never a
barrel of components, never a deep import into another context.

## `src/app/**`

Routing and thin adapters only. A route handler or page parses its input with Zod, calls one use
case, and maps the result to a response. Route handlers stay route handlers; do not convert them
to Server Actions.

## Shared kernel (stays where it is for now)

`src/components/ui/*`, `src/components/layout/*`, `src/lib/logger.ts`, `src/i18n/*`,
`src/lib/db/prisma.ts` and `src/lib/auth/*`. New shared code goes next to them.

## Code that has not moved yet

Contexts move one at a time, `combos` first. Until a context has its `src/modules/<context>/`
folder, its code stays in `src/lib/<area>/`, `src/components/<area>/` and `src/hooks/`. When you
change such code:

- keep the layers apart in place: a pure rule goes in a file with no Prisma, `fetch` or React
  import, and I/O goes in another file;
- never move a file in a behaviour PR. Moves have their own PR and rewrite every import and
  `vi.mock` path in the same commit.

## Everywhere

- Dependencies point inward: `ui` and `client` → `application` → `domain`. `infrastructure`
  implements the ports. `domain` imports only its own context and other contexts' `index.ts`.
- The server owns the rules and never blocks an edit for legality: legality is a report
  (`docs/adr/0009-legality-is-a-report-and-the-server-owns-rules.md`). The browser keeps its
  current feedback checks (the copy clamp, the companion check, "already in the deck") unchanged.
  A new browser check imports the pure function that owns the rule; it never re-implements it.
- A provider's wire type (`ScryfallCard`, a model response) stays in its adapter
  (`docs/adr/0004-anti-corruption-layers-for-external-providers.md`).
- No Prisma in a component, a hook, a store or `domain/`. No new import cycle; the 3 existing
  type-only cycles are recorded in the baseline.

## Enforcement

Today review checks these rules. dependency-cruiser (`pnpm arch`), `server-only` and the ESLint
boundaries come in stages (`docs/adr/0007-architecture-enforcement-in-stages.md`); until they
exist, say in the PR when a change adds to a known violation.
